---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About Flow | Flow by Adapt"
	description="The story behind Flow by Adapt — Revit tools developed from real architectural practice and everyday project workflows."
>
	<section class="about-page">
		<div class="about-intro">
			<p class="eyebrow">ABOUT FLOW</p>

			<h1>
				Built from practice,<br />
				not from a product brief.
			</h1>

			<p class="about-lead">
				Flow grew out of everyday architectural project work —
				solving repetitive tasks, improving consistency and making
				complex Revit workflows easier to manage.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

    <section class="about-story">
	<div class="about-story-inner">
		<div class="about-story-heading">
			<p class="eyebrow">WHERE IT STARTED</p>

			<h2>
				Flow wasn't originally intended
				to become a product.
			</h2>
		</div>

		<div class="about-story-copy">
			<p>
				It began with the same problems architectural teams encounter
				every day: repetitive tasks, inconsistent processes and Revit
				workflows that required more time and attention than they should.
			</p>

			<p>
				The first tools were developed simply to make project work
				better — reducing manual steps, making standards easier to
				maintain and giving designers more reliable ways to complete
				recurring tasks.
			</p>

			<p>
				Over time, those individual solutions began to grow into
				something more connected. Flow developed from that collection
				of practical tools into a broader approach to improving how
				architectural work gets done in Revit.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="about-evolution">
	<div class="about-evolution-inner">
        <div class="about-evolution-heading">
            <p class="eyebrow">HOW IT EVOLVED</p>

            <h2>
                From visual scripts to 
                native Revit tools.
            </h2>

            <div
                class="tech-cloud"
                aria-label="Technologies used during the development of Flow"
            >
                <img
                    src={`${base}/branding/tech/dynamo.png`}
                    alt="Dynamo"
                    class="tech-cloud-icon tech-cloud-icon--dynamo"
                />

                <img
                    src={`${base}/branding/tech/python.png`}
                    alt="Python"
                    class="tech-cloud-icon tech-cloud-icon--python"
                />

                <img
                    src={`${base}/branding/tech/ironpython.png`}
                    alt="IronPython"
                    class="tech-cloud-icon tech-cloud-icon--ironpython"
                />

                <img
                    src={`${base}/branding/tech/pyrevit.png`}
                    alt="pyRevit"
                    class="tech-cloud-icon tech-cloud-icon--pyrevit"
                />

                <img
                    src={`${base}/branding/tech/csharp.png`}
                    alt="C#"
                    class="tech-cloud-icon tech-cloud-icon--csharp"
                />

                <img
                    src={`${base}/branding/tech/dotnet.png`}
                    alt=".NET"
                    class="tech-cloud-icon tech-cloud-icon--dotnet"
                />
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="about-evolution-copy">
            <p>
                Flow's development began with Dynamo — using visual scripting to
                automate repetitive Revit tasks and explore better ways of working.
                As those workflows became more ambitious, development moved into
                Python, initially using IronPython and later CPython 3.
            </p>

            <p>
                That progression allowed increasingly capable tools to be developed,
                but it also exposed the limitations of building larger, more integrated
                workflows around scripting environments.
            </p>

            <p>
                Flow eventually moved to native C#, allowing much deeper integration
                with Revit and providing the foundation for more robust interfaces,
                shared services and connected tools that behave as a cohesive
                application rather than a collection of individual scripts.
            </p>

            <p>
                The technology has changed considerably along the way, but the purpose
                has remained the same: use automation to make everyday architectural
                project work simpler, more consistent and easier to manage.
            </p>
        </div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="about-practice">
    <div class="about-practice-inner">

        <div class="about-practice-heading">
            <p class="eyebrow">BUILT IN PRACTICE</p>

            <h2>
                Developed alongside
                real project work.
            </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="about-practice-copy">
            <p>
                Flow isn't developed in isolation from the workflows it is
                intended to improve. The tools are shaped through everyday
                architectural project work, where they can be tested against
                real models, real deadlines and the practical demands of
                delivering projects in Revit.
            </p>

            <p>
                That connection to practice helps keep the focus on useful,
                practical improvements — reducing unnecessary steps, making
                recurring processes more consistent and helping designers
                spend less time managing the mechanics of their workflow.
            </p>

            <p>
                Flow is intended to fit around and improve the way architectural
                teams already work — not replace it.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>


<section class="about-now">
    <div class="about-now-inner">

        <p class="eyebrow">WHERE IT IS NOW</p>

        <h2>
            One toolkit.<br />
            Built around architectural practice.
        </h2>

        <p class="about-now-lead">
            Flow has grown into a connected suite of Revit tools supporting
            project setup, workspace, content, modelling, documentation and
            model review.
        </p>

        <p class="about-now-copy">
            The scope has grown, but the underlying idea remains the same:
            identify the parts of everyday project work that can be made
            simpler, more reliable or easier to manage, and build practical
            tools around them.
        </p>

        <p class="about-now-copy">
            Flow continues to develop in response to real project workflows.
            New tools and improvements are driven by the same question that
            started it: can this part of the process be made better?
        </p>

        <div class="about-now-actions">
            <a class="about-now-button about-now-button-primary" href={`${base}/tools`}>
                Explore Flow tools
            </a>

            <a
                class="about-now-button about-now-button-secondary"
                href="https://help.flowbyadapt.com/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
                Flow.Help
            </a>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>